1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:11,120
So I took a friend of mine out on his first canoe trip.

3
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:14,980
Never been in a canoe before. I was in the stern, and he was up in the bow.

4
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,980
And we were on a river, and there was some whitewater ahead.

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:19,580
And he looked back at me. He was a little nervous.

6
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:23,320
He says, if the canoe tips over, are we going to fall out?

7
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:27,680
And I said, no, there's no reason we can't continue to be friends.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,380
Are we going to fall out?

9
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:37,940
Okay. All right. Not real funny, but this is podcast number 28.

10
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:38,940
All right.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,800
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster's podcast.

12
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:12,480
This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,260
Hey, thanks for being patient with me last week.

14
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:18,280
Podcast number 27 was a little late getting online.

15
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:22,380
I was at Scout Camp, and, well, I was at Scout Camp.

16
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:30,040
So it took a little bit more than I thought to make sure it was online and got to you in time.

17
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:31,740
Here's an email I received recently.

18
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,360
I just wanted to say thank you to Liberty Frederick.

19
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,200
I just wanted to commend you on your excellent podcast and a tremendous resource for Scouters everywhere.

20
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:47,720
Though your podcast is Boy Scout specific, I have chosen to stick with Cub Scouts and more recently in a district position.

21
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:52,880
The general information you offer in regards to a well-run program is invaluable.

22
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:58,940
I was especially fond of the show in which you described the pitfalls of over-volunteering

23
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:04,820
and also thoroughly enjoyed Podcast 21 in which you discussed recruitment on a Boy Scout level.

24
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:08,419
So thanks for your podcast and for all you do.

25
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,739
Well, thank you, Liberty, for writing in.

26
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:12,220
Sure do appreciate that.

27
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:21,160
And you can email me at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N, at gmail.com.

28
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:27,940
Today in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less, we're going to talk a little bit about troop meetings.

29
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:32,200
And this will probably stretch into two or three different podcasts.

30
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:39,600
But we're going to talk about the role of the Scout Master and his assistants in troop meetings

31
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:43,899
and what troop meetings should look like and what they're meant to achieve.

32
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:52,200
Then moving on from there, I have a Scout Masters minute about our 100th anniversary.

33
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:04,100
And finally, we're going to move into planning for the future, planning for your unit and for you for the future.

34
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:07,019
And that is going to be installment number two of that feature.

35
00:03:07,339 --> 00:03:09,780
So we're going to go ahead and get moving.

36
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:11,299
Lots to take care of.

37
00:03:11,299 --> 00:03:15,519
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

38
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:30,020
The mission of the Boy Scouts of America, I mean the official mission statement, reads this way.

39
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:36,580
It is to prepare young people to make ethical and moral choices over their lifetimes

40
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:41,900
by instilling in them the values of the Scout Oath and law.

41
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,700
That's the official BSA mission statement.

42
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:47,280
I think it puts it very succinctly.

43
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,340
Now, it doesn't mention a product.

44
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:56,900
It mentions a set of competencies and skills that are going to be developed in young people.

45
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:00,180
We're not looking for a model citizen.

46
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:04,700
We're not looking for a leader, even though we talk about leaders an awful lot.

47
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:15,859
And if we were producing some kind of a product, it would make sense to have efficient means of production and quality control,

48
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:17,159
but it's not our mission.

49
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,240
We don't produce a product.

50
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:25,940
Note that the mission statement doesn't say much about leadership and developing leaders.

51
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:33,460
What we do in Scouting is we play a game, and the gist of the game is to offer a set of circumstances

52
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:39,280
that challenge Scouts to think for themselves, to make decisions, and develop skills.

53
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:42,920
This process prepares them for a lifetime of challenges.

54
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:46,200
When they leave us prepared, we've completed the mission.

55
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:49,620
There's no test to measure the results.

56
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:52,640
There's a little anecdotal evidence that we can look at.

57
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:58,400
But when they leave us prepared, we've completed the mission.

58
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:02,380
I harp on this subject a lot.

59
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:06,080
And I do it because I think it's one of the toughest things to get about Scouting.

60
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:08,080
It was one of the toughest things for me to get.

61
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:14,480
Face it, most of us in our professional lives were involved in the business of production,

62
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:20,160
or the science of management, and we're used to having some demonstrably measurable result.

63
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,160
When we become scouters, we're eager to make good,

64
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,520
and so we apply the principles of our professional work to our scouting job.

65
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:34,520
We try to make scouting more efficient.

66
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:37,260
We try and have quality control.

67
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:40,680
We try to have some kind of optimum production practice.

68
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:48,420
And we're frankly frustrated when scouts don't behave like widgets on a production line.

69
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:52,080
I know I have been, and I would guess that you are too.

70
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:59,900
What we need to do is liberate ourselves from being results-oriented

71
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:01,700
and become more process-oriented.

72
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:03,600
Concentrate on the process.

73
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:05,820
Learn and maintain the game.

74
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:11,780
Protect this integrity and keep it open and vibrant, interesting, and challenging.

75
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:19,300
And we need to give our scouts a safe place to play, to win, or to lose sometimes.

76
00:06:20,219 --> 00:06:29,580
We don't look so much for measurable results as for some real intangible things like growth,

77
00:06:30,159 --> 00:06:32,360
like being prepared to meet life's challenges,

78
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:37,960
and having instilled in them those things in the scout oath and law

79
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:43,620
that make them into decent human beings in the end.

80
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:50,900
One of the major ways, one of the major tools that we use as scouters to do this is a troop meeting.

81
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:55,040
So we're going to take a little time and talk about the nature of a troop meeting,

82
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:56,740
what's supposed to happen at a troop meeting,

83
00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:00,020
how things are supposed to unfold, who does what,

84
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:05,219
because I think this is something that we really need to work on.

85
00:07:05,659 --> 00:07:08,219
I know that I'm working on it all the time personally.

86
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:09,960
I'm checking on it.

87
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:11,099
I'm thinking about it.

88
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:19,120
I'm applying a lot of effort in making troop meetings what they are supposed to be.

89
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:22,460
And I think we all spend a lot of time doing that.

90
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:31,880
So that's the basis for the next several installments of Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

91
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:39,299
And I know it's getting to be that time of year where we're beginning to look down the next several weeks

92
00:07:39,299 --> 00:07:43,599
and we see troop meetings are going to be starting up again and things are going to be happening.

93
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:46,080
So I think it's a good time to discuss it.

94
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:49,560
And we'll come back to this subject in the next podcast.

95
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:21,920
So this is the second in our series of talking about the future of your unit.

96
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:26,460
Whether you're leading a Cub Pack, a Scout Troop, or a Venture Crew,

97
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:31,180
hopefully some of the questions that we're working on will be useful to you.

98
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:33,440
This series is based on four questions.

99
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:37,240
And the first of them that we're going to discuss right now is

100
00:08:37,240 --> 00:08:42,520
what is the minimum annual number of new members, both youth and adult, it takes to maintain your unit.

101
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:48,620
Well, let me talk about my troop for a moment because it's just a good way to get started.

102
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:54,880
Our troop has been hovering around 30 to 35 Scouts for many years now.

103
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:57,280
We go slightly below and slightly above.

104
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:01,020
Happy to say that we brought in 16 new Scouts last year.

105
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:05,680
So we're at around 45 to 48 Scouts right now.

106
00:09:05,819 --> 00:09:08,819
They never hold still long enough for me to count them all.

107
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:12,640
I'm sure the troop committee knows exactly how many guys we have.

108
00:09:13,319 --> 00:09:20,680
But so we know that we need to bring in, you know, a certain number of Scouts

109
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:26,060
to maintain the program as we run it every single year.

110
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:36,900
We have probably five or six, over time, five or six very involved, committed, uniformed leaders,

111
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:39,439
the Scoutmaster and Assistant Scoutmasters.

112
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:42,459
These are the ones that we see at every troop meeting.

113
00:09:42,599 --> 00:09:46,880
There's probably another three or four that we don't see at every meeting,

114
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:50,420
but that still maintain an interest in helping out with the troop.

115
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:54,640
And then we have four or five core committee members.

116
00:09:55,380 --> 00:10:03,439
And then we bring parents in two or three times a year to help work out with fundraisers and the like.

117
00:10:05,319 --> 00:10:12,459
We need to recruit five or ten new Scouts every year to maintain our troop over time.

118
00:10:13,099 --> 00:10:16,900
What we say, you know, our little slogan is five to stay alive.

119
00:10:17,079 --> 00:10:18,000
That's our watchword.

120
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:25,100
Every single year we have three or four boys who reach their 18th birthday and they're off to college

121
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:26,680
and we say goodbye to them.

122
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:28,600
I think it was five this year.

123
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:36,580
And by natural attrition we tend to lose, you know, two or three out of ten Scouts within their first year.

124
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:41,680
Once they've been in the troop for a year or more, we tend to lose very few of them.

125
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:46,300
But within that first year, you know, they're leaving the fifth grade, going into the sixth grade.

126
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:51,219
Their interests are changing, their lives are changing, and sometimes they, you know, we don't see them.

127
00:10:51,719 --> 00:10:55,400
And that's a natural attrition, and I don't get too upset about it.

128
00:10:55,760 --> 00:11:00,420
But, you know, it's something that we have learned to expect over time.

129
00:11:01,359 --> 00:11:04,740
We devote a great deal of energy to recruiting every year.

130
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:14,260
One of my assistant Scoutmasters is a former Cub Master, and he is charged with actively communicating with our three or four local Cub Packs

131
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:17,840
and keeping up a very active DEN Chief program.

132
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:21,420
We offer our Cub Packs leader training.

133
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:28,320
We offer assistance to help them with anything that they need help with, anything at all.

134
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:34,860
And that way we keep the door open, and they know what the troop is doing, and we know what the Packs are doing.

135
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:38,840
Now, I won't say that we've always had that because we haven't.

136
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:40,480
And we've gotten caught short.

137
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:47,680
Because for many, many years there, we just had a natural expectation that we would see our five, six, to ten Scouts

138
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:50,680
cross over from Weebelos every single year.

139
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:52,339
And that's the way that it went.

140
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:59,380
And then there would, you know, come a year where February would roll around, and we would realize,

141
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:01,540
wow, we're not doing too well.

142
00:12:01,599 --> 00:12:05,459
We're only going to have one or two boys cross over from Weebelos this year.

143
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:08,000
And then we go into full assault mode on recruiting.

144
00:12:08,699 --> 00:12:11,520
And that's kind of frustrating, and it takes a lot of energy.

145
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:14,219
And it often doesn't yield very much.

146
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:19,960
So here's the way that we've decided to handle things, and the way that I encourage you,

147
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:23,540
just in the way of broad concepts, is you're constantly recruiting.

148
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:28,760
There's not, if you concentrate on one or two big recruiting events a year,

149
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:31,580
either a school night for Scouting or that Weebelos crossover,

150
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:34,700
there's going to come a year where you're going to lose out.

151
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:39,900
Any number of factors can come into play, and all of a sudden you discover,

152
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:42,320
wow, we're not bringing any new boys in.

153
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:49,200
In addition to these, you know, kind of big single events,

154
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:54,860
we have kind of a culture that we've developed with our boys,

155
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:57,640
who are especially in the sixth and seventh grades,

156
00:12:57,820 --> 00:12:59,760
to make sure that they're talking to their friends.

157
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:03,900
You know, we get three or four boys every year that way,

158
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:09,060
and we present whoever recruited them with the recruiter's patch,

159
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:10,480
and we make a big fuss over them.

160
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:14,700
And we encourage our guys to build their patrols through recruiting.

161
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:18,980
We encourage our parents to talk to other parents about Scouts.

162
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:25,980
And you don't have to have, like, this full frontal assault on recruiting

163
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:29,260
if you've always got it on the back burner, if you know what I mean.

164
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:37,460
It just makes life a lot simpler when we have that as a part of the culture of the unit.

165
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:39,040
And that's what I suggest to you.

166
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:42,439
One of the best things that you can do for your unit right now

167
00:13:42,439 --> 00:13:47,400
is to inculcate the idea of a continual recruiting.

168
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:48,780
Continual recruiting.

169
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:54,579
I know what I'm going to do if somebody shows up at a troop meeting with a parent,

170
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:56,839
because that's what we ask them to do.

171
00:13:57,900 --> 00:13:59,439
And I know what I'm going to do.

172
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:01,560
I know I'm going to sit with the parent.

173
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:03,180
I'm going to explain the program to them.

174
00:14:03,319 --> 00:14:06,959
The boys are used to seeing visitors at troop meetings,

175
00:14:06,959 --> 00:14:11,240
so they don't have any problem involving them into the program and things like that.

176
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:14,820
And it seems to work.

177
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,440
It seems to work very well.

178
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:20,760
Now, that being said, we do depend on that big single event,

179
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:26,220
those Weeblos crossing over every year, to supply the majority of our Scouts.

180
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:33,500
But if we concentrated on that alone, we could run into trouble from year to year.

181
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:40,600
We need to add approximately one active, dedicated adult

182
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:43,620
every year or two.

183
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,920
Our typical adult leader tenure is maybe about five or six years.

184
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:53,120
But it's amazing to me that a lot of adults have stayed around

185
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:55,840
well past their son's years with us.

186
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:59,400
Some for 10, some for 15, some for even 20 years.

187
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,740
This is my 26th year as a Scoutmaster.

188
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:08,180
And I'm amazed at the number of people who have hung on

189
00:15:08,180 --> 00:15:10,240
and continue to work with the troop.

190
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:11,800
But I'm very, very thankful for them.

191
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:12,640
I can tell you that.

192
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:18,240
We have to train and nurture our new adults.

193
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:20,300
We have to get them developed.

194
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:22,620
They need to understand the program.

195
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:27,000
And to do that, we work with them, train them.

196
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:32,460
And then we make sure that they're rewarded for their service very simply, annually.

197
00:15:32,460 --> 00:15:37,300
And that we maintain a good cadre of people and that they're happy.

198
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,500
That's all a lot simpler than it sounds, isn't it?

199
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:44,760
I mean, you know, you're going to have problems and things like that.

200
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:52,880
But the broad concept here is, you know, how many new Scouts, how many new adults do you need to put in every year?

201
00:15:54,439 --> 00:15:58,060
You're not waiting until it's an emergency.

202
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:01,140
You're keeping a constant eye on recruiting.

203
00:16:01,979 --> 00:16:07,400
You're not waiting until that den leader says that they're not going to come back next year.

204
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:15,239
You've always got your eye on the bench and who's going to be next at the plate for any given den or anything like that.

205
00:16:15,240 --> 00:16:24,360
Now, the particulars, I don't think are as important as the concept that we are continuously thinking and acting to keep our troop on an even keel.

206
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:27,180
Slipping membership.

207
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:29,480
And like I said, I've been caught before.

208
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,040
We've had those low years.

209
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:34,580
And it's like kind of like getting dehydrated.

210
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:39,440
You know, if you've discovered that you're dehydrated, it's too late.

211
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:48,520
You can't, you know, grab a gallon of water and slosh it down and expect that you're going to be back on an even keel real quickly.

212
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:51,100
It's going to take a couple hours before you're back.

213
00:16:51,380 --> 00:16:53,880
And, you know, you've got to go sit in the shade and drink some water.

214
00:16:54,140 --> 00:16:58,600
And that's the same thing that happens when you've forgotten about membership and you kind of put it aside.

215
00:16:59,180 --> 00:17:03,340
And you're not taking sips of water all the time.

216
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:07,860
And then you think, oh, my gosh, our membership's way down.

217
00:17:07,859 --> 00:17:12,179
Well, then you're off to sit in the shade and drink a gallon of water figuratively.

218
00:17:14,199 --> 00:17:16,959
Membership is a similar ongoing concern.

219
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:21,000
You know, if it becomes a real problem, it's harder to resolve.

220
00:17:21,359 --> 00:17:24,099
It's just much easier if you can maintain it.

221
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:29,199
So the next time we're together, we're going to talk about our second question in this series.

222
00:17:29,419 --> 00:17:34,879
And that is, who are your key unit leaders for the next five years?

223
00:17:37,860 --> 00:18:07,840
We'll be right back.

224
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:24,179
I saw the same blog post that many of you saw from the Smithsonian Institute's Museum of Natural History.

225
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:36,320
One of the curators was peeking into their collection of scouting artifacts and put them online in a way of recognizing the 100th anniversary of scouting.

226
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:41,879
And one I thought was particularly, one of the artifacts was particularly compelling to me.

227
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:48,960
It was a jacket dated between 1911 and 1914.

228
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:52,340
So just a year into the founding of the BSA.

229
00:18:52,700 --> 00:18:56,419
I have one similar to the one that they put on there.

230
00:18:56,560 --> 00:18:57,620
It's quite a bit older.

231
00:18:57,800 --> 00:18:59,840
It's from the mid-20s, I think.

232
00:18:59,980 --> 00:19:03,460
At least that's my best guess, according to the patches that are on it.

233
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:10,980
And what I really liked about that new scout jacket that was, you know, new in 1911,

234
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:18,520
it represented a period of scouting when boys often kind of cobbled together their own uniforms.

235
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:26,139
I don't know for sure whether, like, you could go out and buy a scout uniform in 1911.

236
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:27,540
Perhaps you could.

237
00:19:28,139 --> 00:19:35,700
But I know that boys were so excited about scouting that they kind of put together a scout uniform with whatever they could get.

238
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:41,420
You know, they would just try and get close to the pictures that they saw in the Boy Scout handbook.

239
00:19:41,420 --> 00:19:44,960
And I think it speaks of the eagerness and excitement of the time.

240
00:19:45,180 --> 00:19:50,920
As boys became aware of scouting, it was a new idea and it really sparked their imaginations.

241
00:19:52,180 --> 00:20:00,820
And I got to thinking about it and 100 years later, each of our boys, each of our scouts has a very similar experience.

242
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:06,520
Each one discovers scouting as those boys did a century ago.

243
00:20:06,520 --> 00:20:11,000
To each of our scouts, scouting is a new idea.

244
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:22,660
They continue to respond to the invitation of scouting, to the invitation for adventure, for challenge,

245
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:29,020
with the same eagerness and excitement as those boys did in scouting's early years.

246
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:30,580
We have a great history.

247
00:20:30,580 --> 00:20:33,159
We have long-observed traditions.

248
00:20:34,040 --> 00:20:35,340
But it's all new.

249
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:41,000
It's all new to a boy who discovers scouting for the first time.

250
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:46,100
Our culture may have changed in many ways in the last 100 years.

251
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:49,419
But scouting is still relevant.

252
00:20:49,860 --> 00:20:54,720
And that relevance is renewed in the mind of each boy that becomes a scout.

253
00:20:55,320 --> 00:20:58,620
Because in his eyes, it's a fresh discovery.

254
